    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d263">

                <pb facs="0331" n="215" xml:id="pg215"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 839.51/1258.</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Santo Domingo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-03-05T10:00:00-04:40">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 5, 1914, 10 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In considering solution of financial problem can you loan money against alcohol
                    and tobacco taxes on condition that said taxes shall be collected by
                    Receivership? With the right to collect this tax General Receiver’s hands would
                    be strengthened to the extent that he could aid materially in maintaining law
                    and order by practically policing the country. Vick and I agree that this
                    advantage would be most important in the work of establishing orderly
                    government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sullivan</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
